# NOAA trend analysis handoff

Run `python3 analysis/analyze.py` from the repository; the preserved input hash is verified before fitting. No new download occurs. `analysis/fetch.py` records the download logic used interactively on the first turn (with an added overwrite guard); the original fetch read the URL with `urllib.request.urlopen`, wrote the bytes unchanged, and recorded UTC completion time and SHA-256. Do not replace the dated snapshot with a fresh download when reproducing the results.

## Main results

Using all 66 annual means 1960–2025, x = year − 1992.5, CO2 in ppm:

- Line: 361.936969696970 + 1.685817346832 x; residual SD 4.480704 ppm; RMSE 4.412292 ppm; R² 0.981474.
- Quadratic: 357.073416193182 + 1.685817346832 x + 0.013401295533 x²; residual SD 0.764596 ppm; RMSE 0.747017 ppm; R² 0.999469.
- 1960–1969 average quadratic derivative: 0.935344796994 ppm/year; conventional 95% CI [0.901419028403, 0.969270565585].
- 2016–2025 average quadratic derivative: 2.436289896671 ppm/year; conventional 95% CI [2.402364128080, 2.470215665261].

The derivative b + 2cx averaged over the ten annual labels is its value at the window midpoint (1964.5 / 2020.5); it also equals the endpoint fitted change divided by nine years. These are rates of the trend fitted to the entire sample, not separate ten-year regressions or observed endpoint growth rates. Centering makes the line slope and quadratic midpoint slope identical for this symmetric complete annual sample; this is expected, not a transcription error.

## Intervals and robustness

Headline intervals use s²(XᵀX)⁻¹ from the quadratic OLS residuals, s² = SSE/63, and t(0.975,63) for contrasts [0,1,−56] and [0,1,56]. Full coefficient covariance is retained; slope/curvature covariance happens to be zero because of centering and symmetric sampling. These intervals condition on an adequate quadratic mean and independent homoskedastic errors. They are confidence intervals for the fitted trend derivative, not prediction intervals or NOAA measurement error bars. All source `unc` values are equal (0.12), so inverse-variance weighting would not change the coefficient estimates; using 0.12 as the known total residual SD would incorrectly omit trend departures.

Quadratic residual lag-1 correlation = 0.817329; Durbin–Watson = 0.355982. Independence is not supported. Newey–West/Bartlett coefficient covariance sensitivities at lags 1,3,5,10 (finite-sample n/(n−3) correction, same t multiplier) are in results.json. At lag 5, intervals widen to [0.874507,0.996183] and [2.366367,2.506213] ppm/year. At lag 10: [0.868301,1.002388] and [2.358753,2.513827]. The faster recent fitted growth is insensitive to these bandwidths; exact nominal 95% coverage is not established, and HAC does not remove polynomial misspecification. The report should explicitly label conventional intervals as conditional and mention serial correlation; optional compact lag-5 sensitivity adds useful context.

## Validation and provenance

The fit takes approximately 0.02 seconds after imports (complete run under 3 seconds); no large experiment is needed for a deterministic 66-row regression. Assertions verify the raw hash, finite complete annual sampling, agreement of predictions with an independently parameterized NumPy polynomial fit to 1e−8 ppm, and equality of midpoint derivative and endpoint fitted change/9 to 1e−12. `annual_fits.csv` provides observations, predictions and residuals. `results.json` follows the figure owner's requested schema and adds full covariance, diagnostics, versions and interval sensitivity. No coefficients or input observations are typed into the calculation.

Source caveats: final year is subject to revision; the header describes Maunakea substitution during the 2022–23 Mauna Loa interruption. They are provenance notes, not controlled covariates or causally attributed effects.
